'''amphotis''' (am-fō′tis), ''n.''; pl. ''amphotides'' (am-fot′i-dēz). [< Gr. ἀμφωτις or ἀμφωτίς, < ἀμφί, on both sides, + οὖς (ὠτ-) = E. ''ear''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' In ''Gr. antiq.'', a covering of leather or woolen stuff worn over the ears by boxers.—'''2.''' [''cap.''] [NL.] In ''zoöl.'', a genus of coleopterous insects.

'''Ampithoë''' (am-pith′ọ̄-ē), ''n.'' See ''Amphithoë''.

'''ample''' (am′pl), ''a.'' [< late ME. ''ample'', < F. ''ample'', < L. ''amplus'', prob. < ''am-'' for ''ambi-'', around (see ''ambi-''), + ''-plus'' for *''-pulus'', full, = E. ''full''<sup>1</sup>: cf. L. ''plenus'', full, and see ''full''<sup>1</sup> and ''plenty''.] '''1.''' Large in dimensions; of great size, extent, capacity, or bulk; wide; spacious; extended.
{{smaller block|<poem>All the people in that ''ample'' hous
Did to that image bowe their humble knee.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., III. xi. 49.}}
<poem>Of deeper too and ''ampler'' floods,
Which, as in mirrors, shew’d the woods.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', To Mrs. Anne Killigrew, l. 112.}}
<poem>Her waist is ''ampler'' than her life,
For life is but a span.</poem>
{{right|''O. W. Holmes'', My Aunt.}}}}
'''2.''' Large in kind or degree; having full scope or extent; copious; unrestricted; unrestrained: as, an ''ample'' narrative; to give ''ample'' praise, or do ''ample'' justice.
{{smaller block|<poem>Were I alone to pass the difficulties,
And had as ''ample'' power as I have will.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. and C., ii. 2.}}
The noble and rich may diffuse their ''ample'' charities.
{{right|''Steele'', Guardian, No. 174.}}}}

readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|<poem>To him we grant our ''amplest'' powers to sit
Judge of all present, past, and future wit.</poem>
{{right|''Pope'', Dunciad, ii. 375.}}}}

'''3.''' Fully sufficient for any purpose, or for the purpose specified; abundant; liberal; plentiful: as, ''ample'' provision for the table.
{{smaller block|An ''ample'' number of horses had been purchased in England with the public money.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., xiv.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Give ''ample'' room and verge enough
The characters of Hell to trace.</poem>
{{right|''Gray'', The Bard, ii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|='''Syn.''' ''Ample, Copious, Plenteous'', spacious, roomy, extensive, extended, wide, capacious, abundant, sufficient, full, enough, unrestricted, plenary, unstinted. (See lists under ''abundant'' and ''large''.) ''Ample'', in its more common uses, has reference to the sufficiency of the supply for every need; ''copious'' carries with it the idea of the unfailingness of the source; while ''plenteous'' usually indicates largeness of quantity in actual possession: as, ''ample'' stores or resources; a ''copious'' supply of materials; a ''plenteous'' harvest.}}
{{smaller block|By their [the philosophers’] long career of heroic defeat, they have furnished us with a concrete demonstration, almost superfluously ''ample'', of the relativity of human knowledge.
{{right|''J. Fiske'', Cosmic Philos., I. 26.}}}}
{{smaller block|It [the Union] has been to us all a ''copious'' fountain of national, social, and personal happiness.
{{right|''D. Webster''.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Like over-ripen’d corn,
Hanging the head at Ceres’ ''plenteous'' load.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. VI., i. 2.}}}}

